---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import data from '../data/retail.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const files = `${base}data/india-grocery/`;
const retail = [
  ['pos-lines-atta-june.csv', 'Till lines', 'Every till line for the three atta packs, as scanned: bill, time, item, quantity, price'],
  ['daily-sales-june.csv', 'Daily sales', 'Units and revenue per item per day, as scanned'],
  ['inventory-book-june.csv', 'Book stock', "The system's stock on hand at closing time, per item per day"],
  ['forecasts-june.csv', 'Nightly reorder run', 'Forecast, reorder point, order-up-to level and order for each warehouse item'],
  ['store-orders-june.csv', 'Store orders', 'Orders sent to the warehouse'],
  ['goods-receipts-june.csv', 'Goods receipts', 'Pallets received: what the shipping notice said and what was booked'],
  ['stock-counts-june.csv', 'Stock counts', 'Physical counts and the adjustments they made'],
  ['shelf-audits-june.csv', 'Shelf audits', 'Twice-weekly audits at 11:00: on the shelf or empty'],
  ['write-offs-june.csv', 'Write-offs', 'Damaged and expired stock written off'],
];
const logistics = [
  ['products.csv', 'Products', 'Items, barcodes (GTIN), case sizes and weights, and each item’s slot in the warehouse'],
  ['shipments-june.csv', 'Trucks', 'Each morning’s truck: dispatch, delivery slot and arrival'],
  ['dc-pick-confirmations-june.csv', 'Pick confirmations', 'The warehouse’s record of each order line picked, and how it was confirmed'],
  ['shipping-notice-lines-june.csv', 'Shipping notices', 'What each shipping notice said was on each pallet'],
  ['pallets-june.csv', 'Pallet labels', 'One row per pallet label (SSCC): cases, and the weight its notice implies'],
  ['goods-receipts-june.csv', 'Goods receipts', 'What the store booked, pallet by pallet'],
  ['dc-stock-adjustments-june.csv', 'Warehouse corrections', 'Corrections the warehouse made to its stock of the store’s lines'],
  ['stock-counts-june.csv', 'Store counts', 'Physical counts at the store and the adjustments they made'],
  ['store-orders-june.csv', 'Store orders', 'What the store ordered, to check deliveries against'],
];
const pays = [
  ['pos-payments-june.csv', 'Till payments', 'Every payment attempt at the tills: tender, amount, what the till said, and the UPI reference when the till got one'],
  ['psp-upi-report-june.csv', 'Provider’s UPI report', 'Each UPI payment’s final status from the payment provider, and when it was known'],
  ['upi-settlements-june.csv', 'Settlements', 'The acquirer’s daily UPI settlement: credits, refunds and fees'],
  ['upi-refunds-june.csv', 'Refunds', 'Refunds the store made for UPI payments, and what triggered each one'],
  ['customer-complaints-june.csv', 'Complaints', 'Customer complaints about payments, and how each was resolved'],
];
---
<Layout title="Store 014's June records" description="Synthetic June 2026 records from a fictional Bengaluru supermarket, its warehouse and its payment provider: till lines, book stock, shipping notices, receipts, payments, settlements and complaints, with exercises and answer keys.">
  <h1>Store 014's June records</h1>
  <p class="lede">The records the store's, the warehouse's and the payment provider's systems kept in June 2026: what the tills scanned, what the books said, what was picked, shipped and received, and how every bill was paid. They do not show what was really on the shelf, on the pallets or in customers' accounts. That is the exercise.</p>
  <p>All of it is synthetic and seeded (seed {data.meta.seed}), generated by the India grocery world in the Domain Explorer project at commit <code>{data.meta.commit}</code>. Identifiers use GS1 prefix 29, set aside for restricted circulation, so none is a globally issued product barcode.</p>

  <h2 id="retail-records">Retail: the shelf</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="files">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">What it holds</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {retail.map(([f, name, what]) => (
          <tr><th scope="row"><a href={`${files}${f}`} download>{name}</a><span class="rec">{f}</span></th><td>{what}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h3>Four exercises</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Using only the till lines, find the hours when the 5 kg shelf was probably empty.</li>
    <li>Compare the book stock with sales. When did the book stop moving, and why did nothing get reordered?</li>
    <li>Work out on-shelf availability from the shelf audits, and compare it with the share of days the system showed stock.</li>
    <li>Estimate how many bags of 5 kg atta customers wanted but could not buy in the week of 8 June.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>When you are done, the <a href={`${files}answer-key-retail.json`} download>retail answer key</a> holds what the store could not see: what was really on the shelf each day, every scan error, and the incidents behind the gaps. The <a href={`${base}retail/`}>retail post</a> walks through the story, and the <a href={`${base}demo/#retail`}>demo</a> shows it from each person's side.</p>

  <h2 id="logistics-records">Logistics: the delivery</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="files">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">What it holds</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {logistics.map(([f, name, what]) => (
          <tr><th scope="row"><a href={`${files}${f}`} download>{name}</a><span class="rec">{f}</span></th><td>{what}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h3>Four exercises</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Compute June's on-time-in-full from the trucks, shipping notices, goods receipts and store orders: per delivery, then per order line. Why do the two differ?</li>
    <li>Every record says the 22 June delivery was perfect. Find the evidence, in other tables, that it was not.</li>
    <li>Line up the warehouse's corrections with the store's count adjustments. What do the four have in common?</li>
    <li>Using the case weights in the products file, work out how much lighter the first 22 June pallet was than its notice implies. Would a scale with a 2% tolerance have caught it?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The <a href={`${files}answer-key-logistics.json`} download>logistics answer key</a> holds what was really on every pallet, and OTIF for June measured four ways. The <a href={`${base}logistics/`}>logistics post</a> walks through the story, and the <a href={`${base}demo/#logistics`}>demo</a> shows it from each person's side.</p>
  <h2 id="payments-records">Payments: the money</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="files">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">What it holds</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {pays.map(([f, name, what]) => (
          <tr><th scope="row"><a href={`${files}${f}`} download>{name}</a><span class="rec">{f}</span></th><td>{what}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h3>Four exercises</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Using the till's payments alone, find the hour on 20 June when UPI started failing, and how long it lasted.</li>
    <li>Match the provider's UPI successes to the till's approved payments by reference number. Which credits have no approved payment behind them?</li>
    <li>For each of those credits, find the bill it belongs to and how the customer paid instead. How many customers were charged twice, and for how much?</li>
    <li>How long did each of them wait for a refund? Which ones had not been refunded when June closed?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The <a href={`${files}answer-key-payments.json`} download>payments answer key</a> holds what happened to each customer's money. The <a href={`${base}payments/`}>payments post</a> walks through the story, and the <a href={`${base}demo/#payments`}>demo</a> shows it from each person's side.</p>
  <p class="muted">All files are also described in the folder's <a href={`${files}README.md`}>README</a>.</p>
</Layout>

<style>
  .files th { font-family: var(--font-sign); font-weight: 500; white-space: normal; }
  .files th .rec { display: block; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .files td { white-space: normal; }
</style>
